# Maintainer: Genesi OS Team <dev@genesios.org>

pkgname=genesi-ai-mode
pkgver=1.0.0
# 2026-06-07 (53): Turbo fixes for weak/CPU hardware + working speculative
# decoding. (1) CPU-only machines no longer keep the speculative draft model —
# a second model on the same cores made Turbo SLOWER than stock on an 8GB
# CPU-only laptop. (2) On GPU, the draft is now offloaded with -ngld 999 so it
# runs on the GPU instead of the CPU (the reason spec decoding "never worked").
# (3) CPU path uses f16 KV + auto flash-attn (q8_0 KV + forced FA only help on
# GPU) and pins threads to the fast/perf cores (skips Intel hybrid E-cores).
# 2026-06-07 (54): (1) Monitor chat now hits llama-server /v1/chat/completions
# (applies the model's chat template + stop tokens) instead of /completion with
# a raw prompt — fixes the AI rambling infinite garbage in Turbo. (2) Turbo
# queries nvidia-smi for VRAM when the daemon's state.json has none, so it
# actually offloads to the GPU instead of silently running on CPU. (3) genesi-aid
# re-detects the GPU every ~30s (it was profiled once at boot, before the NVIDIA
# driver was ready) + DRM fallback — fixes the dashboard showing "no GPU".
# 2026-06-07 (55): vendor-agnostic VRAM detection. nvidia-smi doesn't work under
# nouveau/NVK (the live ISO driver), so VRAM read as 0 → Turbo thought the model
# "spilled VRAM", dropped the draft and ran on CPU, and the dashboard said "no
# GPU" — all on a machine whose 3050 llama.cpp clearly sees via Vulkan. Now both
# genesi-aid and genesi-ai-turbo size the GPU via `llama-server --list-devices`
# (the same Vulkan backend used for inference) when nvidia-smi/sysfs can't, so
# spec decoding actually engages on the GPU and the advisor reports the card.
# 2026-06-07 (56): version-proof Turbo flags + CPU speculative decoding. Recent
# llama.cpp removed --draft (now --spec-draft-n-max), so the draft would crash
# the server the moment spec decoding engaged on the GPU; we now read the
# installed binary's --help and pick the flag it actually supports. Also enable
# model-free n-gram speculative decoding (--spec-type ngram-simple) on CPU-only
# machines (no draft model, no extra RAM) so GPU-less laptops get a speculative
# speedup too. Disable with GENESI_TURBO_NO_NGRAM=1.
# 2026-06-07 (57): actually ENGAGE speculative decoding. Recent llama.cpp loads
# the draft model from -md but does nothing unless --spec-type selects an
# implementation (defaults to 'none' -> "no implementations specified for
# speculative decoding"), so the draft burned VRAM with zero speedup. Now we add
# --spec-type draft-simple whenever a draft is used (when the binary supports it).
# 2026-06-07 (58): VRAM-aware offload (Tier 0). Instead of a blind -ngl 999 that
# OOMs a small/partially-full card, use -ngl auto (fit as many layers as the VRAM
# holds) when the model doesn't fully fit; keep 999 when it fits entirely.
# 2026-06-07 (59): FIX REGRESSION — `serve` defaulted to speculative decoding,
# which REGRESSED real-world speed on Vulkan/NVK (plain GPU offload was 21 t/s,
# spec dropped it to ~baseline: the draft + verification cost more than it saves
# without a mature CUDA driver). Default `serve` is back to plain full GPU
# offload (the proven win); speculative decoding is now opt-in (GENESI_TURBO_SPEC
# =1 or an explicit draft). `bench` still compares both so spec can be evaluated.
# 2026-06-07 (60): Lote B — named intensity profiles (2.9). New
# `genesi-ai-mode profile max|balanced|battery|auto` + /run profile file the
# daemon reads live (re-applies on change) + segmented control in the Monitor.
# max=full(governor+EPP+power+GPU), balanced=governor+mem+hogs only,
# battery=hogs+mem (governor untouched), auto=AC->max/battery->battery.
# 2026-06-07 (61): Turbo speculative decoding is now a USER CHOICE (still off by
# default). `genesi-ai-turbo --spec/--speculative` flag, and a "offload total ⇄
# ⚡ speculative" toggle on the Monitor's Turbo card (setTurbo carries the spec
# flag → serve --spec). So users can run Turbo with or without speculation.
# 2026-06-07 (62): 2.8.4 — pin AI processes to the performance cores. On hybrid
# Intel CPUs (P/E) genesi-aid sets the AI process CPU affinity to the P-cores
# (+ HT siblings) so inference never lands on the slow E-cores; restored on
# disable. No-op on uniform CPUs and VMs.
# 2026-06-08 (63): 2.9 — benchmark button in the Monitor. The Painel now has a
# "Benchmark de desempenho" card that wraps `genesi-ai-mode bench [model]`: it
# streams each step live (warming up / OFF / ON), then draws an OFF-vs-ON
# tokens/s bar graph with the % gain. Backend (genesi_ai_monitor.py) runs the
# CLI in a worker thread and parses its output (single source of truth stays the
# CLI; no re-implemented measurement). Uses the active/Turbo/first-installed
# model. Note: on a VM the governor is a no-op so expect ~0% — the card says so.
# 2026-06-08 (64): Tier 1 — predictive preload at login/idle (formalize
# maybe_warm_idle). genesi-aid now persists an MRU of the model weight files the
# user actually loaded to /var/lib/genesi-ai-mode/warm.json (new service
# StateDirectory=genesi-ai-mode, survives reboot). On a fresh boot, while idle,
# the daemon seeds _warm_files from that MRU and warms the LAST-USED model into
# the page cache — so the FIRST prompt after login loads from RAM, not disk
# (previously it only warmed the model after you'd already run it once that
# session). Same conservative gates: throttled, skipped on RAM-backed fs, <50%
# of available RAM (no-op on tight 6 GB VMs). Pure reclaimable cache; nothing to
# restore. Best-effort writes never raise into the main loop.
# 2026-06-08 (65): Lote C — advanced Turbo stack (mostly tied to ⚡ speculative).
# (1) Dynamic draft length: when speculative decoding is ON (a draft model is
# used), genesi-ai-turbo adds a draft-length floor + acceptance-probability
# threshold so llama.cpp adapts how many tokens it drafts to the live acceptance
# rate (each flag gated on --help, names vary by version; env GENESI_TURBO_DRAFT
# _MAX/MIN/P). (2) Persistent prompt/KV cache to disk: --slot-save-path (per-user
# cache dir, gated, GENESI_TURBO_NO_SLOT_CACHE=1 to disable) enables llama-server
# slot save/restore. (3) Advisor → Turbo model pick: `genesi-ai-turbo recommend`
# prints the biggest model that fits 100% in VRAM; the Monitor shows it as a
# one-click "Recomendado p/ sua GPU" chip. (4) Monitor now makes EXPLICIT that
# the ⚡ toggle enables speculative decoding + dynamic draft + persistent KV
# (card text + tooltips). Spec stays OFF by default (still 🔴 BLOCKED on Vulkan/
# NVK); these wins need validation on a real CUDA install via `bench`.
# 2026-06-08 (66): more roadmap items. (1) 2.8.7 sched-ext: opt-in advanced.conf
# `sched_ext = on|<scheduler>[:mode]` loads a BPF sched_ext scheduler in "server"
# (throughput) mode via scxctl on the aggressive path and switches back to what
# ran before on disable; capability-gated on a sched_ext kernel + scxctl (CachyOS
# scx-scheds), silent no-op otherwise. (2) 2.8.3 pre-cache the most-recently-used
# models (plural): the predictive-preload MRU now warms several recent models'
# weight files newest-first while the total stays under ~40% of free RAM. (3) Doc
# only: marked 2.2 (partial offload via -ngl auto, mlock via GENESI_TURBO_MLOCK,
# compositor trim via genesi-ai-kwin-helper) and 2.3 (explicit huge pages on
# demand via advanced.conf) as already-done; rewrote the 2.8.10 speculative-
# decoding item from BLOCKED to a user-toggleable option (no longer a blocker).
# 2026-06-08 (67): Monitor backend install now offers CUDA vs Vulkan with a
# hardware-based recommendation. Clicking "Instalar Backend" opens a dialog that
# explains both and recommends one: CUDA when nvidia-smi works (NVIDIA + the
# proprietary/open driver loaded, ~1.5–2× faster), Vulkan otherwise (universal,
# the shipped genesi-llama-cpp, runs on any GPU incl. nouveau/NVK). Vulkan
# installs genesi-llama-cpp via pkexec; CUDA builds llama.cpp-cuda via the user's
# AUR helper (paru/yay) — best-effort, with clear warnings when nvidia-smi is
# down or no AUR helper exists. New backend: backendInfo()/backendAdvice signal +
# installTurboBackend(kind). Our backend stays Vulkan by default.
# 2026-06-08 (68): real-world live-ISO fixes. (1) Widget "Abrir AI Mode Monitor"
# did nothing: the plasmoid launched it via the executable engine, which kills
# the child when the popup closes (and PATH wasn't guaranteed). Now launches
# detached with an absolute path (`setsid -f /usr/local/bin/genesi-ai-monitor`).
# (2) Monitor "baixar modelo"/chat/loadModels failed with "Errno 111 Connection
# refused" when ollama was installed but its service wasn't running. The Monitor
# now auto-starts ollama (systemctl start, then a user `ollama serve` fallback)
# before talking to the API, with a clear message if it still can't.
# 2026-06-08 (69): standardize ALL user-facing strings to English (the project
# default). Translated the AI Mode Monitor UI (Main.qml, ChatPage.qml,
# AdvisorPage.qml, ChatBubble.qml) + every backend status/error message in
# genesi_ai_monitor.py, the plasmoid widget strings, and genesi-ai-turbo's
# `serve` terminal output. No behavior change — text only.
# 2026-06-09 (70): 2.6 follow-up — SMART auto-detect (no more "latched ON
# forever"). genesi-aid drove AI Mode off `bool(ai_processes)`, but `ollama
# serve` is a persistent daemon → the process is ALWAYS present → AI Mode never
# powered back down and the box sat in performance/locked-GPU-clocks at idle.
# Now it classifies the live workload: active (GPU/CPU crunching, or a one-shot
# CLI) → full "max"; warm (a model is resident via ollama keep_alive / a Turbo
# server, but idle) → eases to "balanced" (governor stays, GPU clock/power locks
# released); idle (server listening, nothing loaded) → powers down. Hysteresis
# prevents flapping between chat turns: instant promote on activity, a 30s hold
# before max→balanced, and an `ai_idle_timeout` (advanced.conf, default 90s)
# grace before standing down. New state.json field `activity`; the Monitor shows
# generating / warm·idle / standing-by under the AI Mode status. Forced ON/OFF
# and explicit profiles still win. Pure detection change — all the optimization
# and reversible-restore machinery is untouched.
# pkgrel 71: reliable Turbo on/off. `free_turbo_port` (CLI) and the Monitor's
# `_stop_turbo` now SIGTERM the llama-server, WAIT for the socket to actually
# release (polling /health / the port), then escalate to SIGKILL — so toggling
# Turbo off then on no longer hits "already running" with a stuck server. The
# Monitor also kills a stray Turbo it didn't start (e.g. one launched from a
# terminal), which our tracked-PID-only stop used to leave running.
# pkgrel 72: the Monitor now ASKS which model to run Turbo on. Flipping the
# Turbo switch on opens a model picker (installed models, the GPU-fit pick
# pre-selected) instead of silently starting on whatever Ollama happened to
# have loaded. The chosen model is locked, so the live activeModel poll can no
# longer overwrite it mid-session (the "Turbo started with a model I didn't
# pick" bug). Turning Turbo off releases the lock.
# pkgrel 73: stop dragging full Plasma onto every install (the Hyprland-installs-
# KDE bug). plasma-workspace was a HARD dep only to host the optional Plasma
# widget and to pull kirigami transitively for the standalone Monitor. But
# genesi-ai-mode is in the always-on "Genesi OS" netinstall group, so that hard
# dep installed plasma-workspace (=> /usr/bin/plasmashell) on EVERY target,
# including a Hyprland-only pick — which then ALSO made genesi-set-session.sh
# detect plasmashell and default the session to Plasma X11. Now depend on
# `kirigami` directly (the only hard runtime need of the Monitor's
# `import org.kde.kirigami`) and demote plasma-workspace to an optdepend (the
# Plasma 6 widget; KDE installs already get it via the KDE-Desktop group). A
# Hyprland install no longer pulls plasmashell, so it boots Hyprland and the
# Kirigami Monitor app still works.
# pkgrel 74: visual identity pass on the Monitor — drop the placeholder emoji
# (🤖🧑⚠💬🦙⚡🚀👇) for a crafted Genesi SVG icon set (monitor/icons/): chat
# avatars (bot/user/alert), Turbo/Ollama mode badges (bolt/chip), the empty-
# state chat glyph, and a rocket in the INFERENCE gauge. GaugeArc gained an
# optional `icon` property (backward-compatible). Adds qt6-svg so QtQuick Image
# rasterises them.
# pkgrel 75: neutral system-dark surfaces in the Monitor (Theme.qml + chat
# bubbles) — green is now an accent only, not the whole background — and a
# StartupWMClass on the .desktop so the taskbar groups it as Genesi, not python3.
# pkgrel 76: Monitor surfaces now use the Genesi system blue (window colour)
# instead of the neutral grey, matching the rest of the desktop.
# pkgrel 77: (1) the Monitor no longer hard-crashes on launch inside a VM. The
# guest GL stack lies and Qt Quick's RHI SIGSEGV'd creating the GL context
# (DrKonqi: QSGRhiSupport::createRhi -> driCreateContextAttribs); genesi_ai_
# monitor.py now falls back to QT_QUICK_BACKEND=software when systemd-detect-virt
# reports a VM (bare metal keeps GPU rendering), and GaugeArc's Canvas renders
# through a CPU image instead of a GPU FBO. (2) AI Mode now also removes window
# TRANSPARENCY, not just blur. Genesi's glass comes from the window-background
# alpha in the color scheme (frosted by blur), which unloading effects can't
# touch, so the helper additionally swaps to a new alpha-free "Genesi-Solid"
# scheme (shipped here) while AI Mode is ON — windows go genuinely solid — and
# restores the previous scheme on off, leaving normal-mode glass exactly as it
# was. (3) de-green the Monitor: its cards/gauges used a dark-green surface;
# they now use the Genesi system blue so it matches the rest of the desktop.
# pkgrel 78: the Monitor still crashed on launch — not GL this time but the
# Darkly style. The org.kde.desktop Qt Quick Controls style delegates drawing to
# the system QStyle (Darkly), and Darkly6 SIGSEGVs when driven from QML (it
# dereferences the null QWidget* qqc2-desktop-style passes to drawControl). So
# the Monitor (and Sandboxes) never opened once that style was active. Switched
# the QQC2 style to Fusion (self-drawn, no QStyle) — dark via QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME
# =kde — which sidesteps the Darkly bug entirely. (pkgrel-77's VM software backend
# is kept; it fixed the earlier GL crash that sat in front of this one.)
# pkgrel 79: AI Mode windows stayed see-through despite pkgrel-77's work. The
# kwin helper unloaded "kwin4_effect_translucency", but KWin's Effects DBus knows
# that effect by its SHORT id "translucency" (the kwin4_effect_ prefix only
# exists in kwinrc) — so isEffectLoaded() returned false, nothing was unloaded,
# and the translucency effect stayed on. Diagnosed live: scheme swap to
# Genesi-Solid worked + blur unloaded, but translucency=true. Fixed the id (now
# unloads both spellings, the bogus one is just skipped).
# pkgrel 80: drop the color-scheme swap entirely (and the Genesi-Solid scheme).
# Live testing proved it useless: Darkly bakes a window's translucency at
# CREATION from the scheme alpha, and swapping the scheme can't un-bake an
# already-open window — AI Mode windows stayed see-through no matter what.
# Genesi's glass now comes solely from the KWin `translucency` effect (windows
# are painted opaque by genesi-settings), which IS a compositor effect and so
# toggles live for every window. The helper just unloads/reloads
# blur+contrast+translucency — unloading = instant full opacity, desktop-wide.
# pkgrel 81: KEEP the glass in AI Mode. The kwin helper now manages NO effects by
# default (empty GENESI_KWIN_EFFECTS) — it's a guarded no-op. Rationale: the
# compositor-trim lever was proven worthless by the bench (ON vs OFF 78.2 vs 77.3
# tok/s = noise; a KWin effect only does GPU work on screen damage, and inference
# leaves the screen near-static), while the half-trimmed result looked ugly — blur
# dropped but windows stayed see-through (transparent-without-frost). Keeping the
# glass fully intact kills that visual bug at ~zero perf cost. Opacity-on-AI-Mode
# is still opt-in via GENESI_KWIN_EFFECTS="blur,contrast,translucency".
# pkgrel 82: Monitor UI polish — the shared GlassCard gained shader-free depth
# (top sheen + bottom shade), an animated hover lift, and a hover brighten. Kept
# SHADER-FREE on purpose so it still renders under QT_QUICK_BACKEND=software in
# VMs. Same GlassCard upgrade landed in genesi-sandboxes + genesi-netinspect.
# pkgrel 83: Theme.qml + GlassCard.qml are no longer kept in monitor/ — they're
# bundled at build from the new canonical genesi-ui-kit/ (one source for the
# shared QML kit, copied in via a sibling ../ ref; NOT a published package, so it
# doesn't trip the pipeline's no-inter-package-dep rule). No runtime change.
# pkgrel 84: shared kit is now ADAPTIVE (follows the system light/dark scheme via
# Kirigami.Theme). The dark branch is byte-identical to before, so on Genesi's
# default dark scheme the Monitor is unchanged; a light system would render it
# light (untested — Monitor light pass TODO).
# pkgrel 85: ship genesi-ai-tray — a standalone SNI system-tray applet so the AI
# Mode widget exists OUTSIDE Plasma (the Hyprland + caelestia-shell session has no
# plasmashell, so the plasmoid can't load there). Pure PyQt6 (no Plasma import);
# left-click pops a frameless panel mirroring the plasmoid (live CPU/RAM/GPU bars,
# status, Force ON/Auto/Force OFF, Open Monitor), right-click is a quick menu. It
# registers as a StatusNotifierItem, so it shows in the caelestia bar's tray (and
# the KDE tray, but KDE keeps using the plasmoid). Started from the caelestia
# hyprland.conf via exec-once (Hyprland ignores /etc/xdg/autostart). The panel is
# theme-adaptive: every surface/text colour is derived from QApplication.palette()
# (qt6ct/kdeglobals — same colour-scheme source the Kirigami Monitor reads) and
# re-applied live on a palette change, so it follows the system light/dark scheme
# (only the brand green stays constant). New dep: python-pyqt6 (the tray's
# toolkit; already on every install via genesi-update).
# pkgrel 86: fix genesi-ai-tray crashing on launch (core-dumped, no icon). My
# theme() used QApplication.palette().color(QPalette.ColorRole.Window); on the
# installed PyQt6 the overloaded color() rejected the scoped ColorRole enum
# ("argument 1 has unexpected type 'ColorRole'") and the palette-change handler
# threw, aborting the process. Switched to QPalette's no-arg convenience brush
# accessors (p.window().color(), p.windowText().color(), …) which sidestep the
# overload entirely; muted is now derived by blending. Theme-adaptive behaviour
# is unchanged.
# pkgrel 87: fix a SECOND genesi-ai-tray crash that pkgrel 86 exposed —
# RecursionError on launch. _apply_theme() calls setStyleSheet(), which itself
# emits a PaletteChange; the changeEvent handler re-applied the theme on that,
# re-entering setStyleSheet → infinite recursion (pkgrel 86's color() fix let
# _apply_theme run far enough to trigger it). Removed the changeEvent handler;
# the theme is applied on construction and re-applied each time the popup opens
# (popup_at), which still tracks a system light/dark switch between opens.
# pkgrel 88: ship genesi-ai-tray.desktop (NoDisplay) matching the tray's app_id.
# Without it Qt logged "Could not register app ID: App info not found for
# 'genesi-ai-tray'" and the SNI host had no app info to resolve the icon — a
# likely reason the tray icon never appeared in the caelestia bar.
# pkgrel 89: the Monitor can now SWITCH the Turbo backend (CUDA <-> Vulkan) after
# install, not only pick one when none exists. installTurboBackend used to
# early-return "already installed" whenever ANY llama-server was present, so a
# Vulkan install blocked ever trying CUDA. Now it only short-circuits when the
# REQUESTED backend is the active one; otherwise it switches (the Vulkan path
# -Rdd-removes a present llama.cpp-cuda first, since both provide llama.cpp). New
# _installed_backend() detects cuda/vulkan via pacman -Qq; the "Backend" button
# in the Turbo card is now always visible (not just when missing) so you can open
# the CUDA/Vulkan chooser any time.
# pkgrel 90: apps follow the SYSTEM accent/colour, no more fixed brand blue. The
# shared kit (Theme.qml/GlassCard.qml/GButton.qml) now derives accent from
# Kirigami.Theme.highlightColor and surfaces from the system background — so the
# Monitor matches the Plasma scheme on KDE and caelestia's Material You palette on
# Hyprland (mirrored into qt6ct by genesi-caelestia-theme-sync). The AI-Mode-only
# components ChatBubble.qml/GaugeArc.qml were also de-hardcoded the same way
# (system bg/accent/text; only error-red + Turbo-orange stay fixed). Rebuild to
# ship the rebundled kit + retheme.
# pkgrel 91: redesign pass + i18n. New left icon SIDEBAR for navigation (replaces
# the top pill-tabs), and a language switcher (EN default / PT-BR, live, persisted)
# in the header — both driven by the new shared genesi-ui-kit I18n.qml (bundled in
# like Theme/GlassCard). All Monitor dashboard strings now go through i18n.t().
# Logic (Turbo/benchmark/advisor) untouched.
# pkgrel 92: finish the i18n pass — the shared I18n instance is now threaded into
# ChatPage + AdvisorPage (so the language switch stays in sync across pages), and
# the Chat/Models pages, the backend/model-picker dialogs and tooltips all go
# through i18n.t() too. Dictionary expanded (chat/advisor/dialog keys).
# pkgrel 93: fix genesi-ai-tray NEVER appearing in the caelestia (Hyprland) bar.
# It was the last PyQt6 QSystemTrayIcon applet, and Qt 6.11's SNI is broken with
# the Quickshell host — it registers but never serves its properties, so the icon
# never shows (this is the "sibling [that] crashed fatally in the DBus path"
# called out in genesi-containers pkgrel 6). Rewrote it on libayatana-appindicator
# (Gtk), the proven SNI path the containers tray already uses: same daemon state +
# on/auto/off + Open Monitor, now as live menu rows (appindicator is menu-only).
# Deps swap python-pyqt6 -> python-gobject + libayatana-appindicator + gtk3 (the
# Monitor uses pyside6, not pyqt6, so nothing else needed pyqt6).
# pkgrel 94: faster Turbo on CUDA + CUDA-backend swap unblocked. (1) genesi-ai-turbo
# now AUTO-enables speculative decoding when the installed llama-server is the CUDA
# build (the proven win on a mature driver) and keeps it OFF on Vulkan/NVK (where it
# can regress) — new backend_is_cuda() probes `llama-server --list-devices` for a
# CUDA device. Pin with GENESI_TURBO_SPEC=1 / GENESI_TURBO_NO_SPEC=1 or --spec/--no-
# spec; it still self-drops the draft when the target spills VRAM, so it only engages
# when it helps. (2) Monitor defaults the ⚡ toggle ON the first time it detects a CUDA
# backend (sticky after that). (3) The VRAM-spill warning now tells the user the model
# is partly on CPU and points at `recommend` / a smaller quant (so a 14B on an 8 GB
# card is explained, not silently slow). NOTE: the CUDA backend swap itself is
# unblocked by genesi-desktop pkgrel 3, which depends on the `llama.cpp` provide
# instead of the `genesi-llama-cpp` name (so llama.cpp-cuda can replace it).
# pkgrel 95: roadmap 2.8.11 push — the "big brain, small-card" batch. (1) MoE
# expert-offload: genesi-ai-turbo reads the GGUF header (new stdlib gguf_meta())
# to detect Mixture-of-Experts models and, when one spills VRAM, keeps ALL
# attention on the GPU (-ngl 999) and pushes only the expert tensors to CPU RAM
# (--n-cpu-moe K, K computed from VRAM via expert_count/block_count; env
# GENESI_TURBO_NCMOE). Only a few experts fire per token, so a ~30B-A3B runs at
# ~7B speed on an 8 GB card — the out-of-the-box win no OS ships by default.
# (2) Advisor `recommend` now also prints a MoE "power pick" (gpt-oss:20b /
# qwen3:30b-a3b) gated on system RAM. (3) ZRAM AI swap (Tier 4 "Ideia 3"):
# genesi-aid brings up a high-priority zstd zram swap on the aggressive path so
# memory pressure compresses in RAM instead of freezing on SSD swap; opt-in
# advanced.conf `zram_ai`, fully reversible (swapoff+reset). (4) always_warm_turbo
# now warms the advisor's VRAM-fit model instead of a hardcoded llama3.2:latest.
# See ROADMAP 2.8.11 Tier 2/4 (microarch auto-dispatch + hardware auto-tune also
# marked done; Medusa/EAGLE documented as upstream-blocked, MoE is its stand-in).
# pkgrel 96: roadmap 2.8.11 Tier 1 — always-warm shared Turbo server as a REAL
# systemd unit. New genesi-turbo.service keeps one llama-server warm on :11435 for
# every Genesi app to reuse (the model resolved via `recommend`, override with
# /etc/genesi-ai-mode/turbo.env GENESI_WARM_MODEL). OFF by default (shipped, not
# enabled). genesi-aid's manage_always_warm now drives this unit (systemctl
# start/stop) instead of spawning a child process — single owner of :11435, no
# port race, survives a daemon restart, restarts on failure (StartLimit-guarded).
# pkgrel 97: three fixes from real bare-metal testing. (1) TIME_WAIT port bug —
# toggling Turbo OFF then ON within ~1-2 min failed as "already running" and told
# the user to pkill a port with nothing on it: port_busy() bind()'d without
# SO_REUSEADDR, so the just-closed socket lingering in TIME_WAIT looked "busy".
# Now sets SO_REUSEADDR -> True only for a LIVE listener. (2) Turbo crashing on
# models ollama can run (e.g. a MoE whose --n-cpu-moe trips our llama-server's
# loader: "wrong number of tensors") — `serve` now degrades gracefully via a
# fallback ladder (optimised -> no-MoE -> plain offload), so it lands on what
# ollama does instead of dying; new GENESI_TURBO_NO_MOE escape hatch. (3) Turbo ==
# ollama for fitting models now that ollama uses the GPU is EXPECTED (same engine);
# Turbo's edge is speculative decoding + MoE — expanded the draft catalogue
# (gemma3->gemma3:1b, gemma2->gemma2:2b, family-exact so tokenizers match) so spec
# actually fires for more models and Turbo is genuinely faster than plain ollama.
# pkgrel 98: out-of-the-box decode wins + CPU tuning. (1) n-gram/prompt-lookup
# speculation now runs on the GPU too (was CPU-only): for a fitting model with NO
# same-family draft (mistral/gemma1/phi) Turbo speculates from the context's own
# n-grams — free, no VRAM, identical output, big on code/agent/RAG/edit where the
# output copies the input. This is the real Turbo edge over plain ollama-on-GPU,
# and the engine behind "retrieval speculation" (MemPalace injects a past answer
# into context → n-gram picks it up). Disable: GENESI_TURBO_NO_NGRAM=1. (2) CPU
# tuning: split thread counts — --threads = physical/perf cores for DECODE,
# --threads-batch = all logical cores for PREFILL (compute-bound, SMT helps) —
# plus --prio 2 to cut scheduler jitter. Gated on --help, env-tunable,
# GENESI_TURBO_NO_CPU_TUNE=1 to skip. (3) Opt-in self-draft (GENESI_TURBO_SELF_
# DRAFT=1): when no family draft exists, llama-quantize builds a cached Q2 of the
# model itself as the draft (modest ~1.1-1.3x, both must fit VRAM so usually a
# no-op on 8 GB — helps bigger cards / smaller targets). ROADMAP: marked n-gram-on-
# GPU done; added PowerInfer (activation sparsity), continuous batching on the warm
# daemon, and true REST retrieval-decode as research items.
# pkgrel 99: spill-spec — Turbo's biggest lever over plain ollama for a model too
# big for the card. OPT-IN, CUDA only (GENESI_TURBO_SPILL_SPEC=1): keep the tiny
# GPU-resident draft EVEN when the target spills VRAM. The spilled layers stream
# over the slow PCIe bus once per forward pass; speculative verification checks K
# draft tokens per pass, so K accepted tokens cost ~1 PCIe weight-stream instead
# of K — amortization plain autoregressive ollama can't do. Default behaviour
# unchanged (still drops the draft when spilling), so this can't regress anyone
# who doesn't set the flag; user benches it on the real card before we consider
# making it default. (n-gram-on-GPU + graceful MoE fallback + expanded draft
# catalogue already shipped in 97/98.)
# pkgrel 100: three OS-wide inference wins (benefit EVERY Genesi app on the shared
# Turbo :11435, not just the Monitor). (1) CONTINUOUS BATCHING — one loaded model
# serves N concurrent slots (--parallel, auto by VRAM headroom, GENESI_TURBO_
# PARALLEL to pin), so Genesi Code's agents + other apps + the new prefill warm
# requests share the SAME weights and decode in parallel: aggregate tokens/s
# scales ~with N. Each slot keeps full per-request context (total KV = ctx*N),
# raised above 1 only for a fully-offloaded model with headroom (spilling/CPU
# stays 1). (2) NIGHTLY AUTOTUNER — new genesi-ai-tune + .timer sweeps ubatch/
# flash-attn/KV-type/threads with llama-bench on the REAL model+hardware and
# writes the fastest config to /var/lib/genesi-ai-mode/tune.json, which
# server_cmd reads as its defaults (env still overrides; GENESI_TURBO_NO_TUNE=1
# to ignore). Self-gates on AC+idle, Nice=15/idle-IO, never steals a live GPU.
# (3) PREFILL-AS-YOU-TYPE — the Monitor warms the KV prefix while you type (see
# genesi_ai_monitor.py) so TTFT is ~0 on send; the pattern is a plain cache_prompt
# call any Genesi app can adopt. New knobs: GENESI_TURBO_FA / GENESI_TURBO_KV to
# override flash-attn/KV type (autotuner uses these). New hidden `genesi-ai-turbo
# gguf-path <tag>` resolves a model file for the tuner.
# pkgrel 101: CUDA backend made a fast one-click, plus a proactive offer. The
# biggest tokens/s lever on NVIDIA (~1.5-2x + auto-spec) was gated behind a
# ~40-min AUR source build nobody waited for, so users silently stayed on Vulkan
# (Turbo ~= ollama). Now: (1) the Monitor's CUDA button installs the PREBUILT
# genesi-llama-cpp-cuda from the [genesi] repo via pkexec pacman (seconds),
# falling back to the AUR llama.cpp-cuda build only if the prebuilt isn't
# published yet; _installed_backend() recognizes the prebuilt as 'cuda' and the
# Vulkan swap removes it too. (2) New genesi-ai-cuda-notify login autostart pops
# ONE desktop notification on NVIDIA+proprietary-driver boxes still on Vulkan,
# opening the Monitor on click (self-contained, never touches genesi-aid, shows
# once, silent on AMD/Intel/nouveau). The prebuilt package + its dispatch CI
# (test-llama-cpp-cuda.yml) ship in genesi-arch/packages/genesi-llama-cpp-cuda;
# wire it into publish-packages.yml only after that dispatch build goes green.
# pkgrel 102: make Turbo genuinely beat non-Turbo, from on-hardware results.
# CONFIRMED on an RTX 3050: CUDA+spec on a FITTING model 40->80 tok/s (2x);
# spill-spec on a SPILLING qwen2.5:14b 9.0->11.7 tok/s (+29%, 37/54 draft
# accepted). Acting on that: (1) spill-spec is now DEFAULT ON for CUDA (was
# opt-in) — keeps the tiny GPU draft when the target spills so speculative
# verification amortizes the slow PCIe weight reads; disable with GENESI_TURBO_
# NO_SPILL_SPEC (still opt-in on Vulkan/NVK). (2) NEW Genesi Fit (GENESI_TURBO_
# FIT=1): when a model would SPILL, requantize it down to the best quant that
# fits 100% in VRAM (llama-quantize, cached) and serve THAT — full-GPU speed
# beats a spilling higher quant, and spec stacks on top; a spilling-model warning
# now advertises it. (3) Fixed the no-spec regression: auto continuous-batching
# was too aggressive (up to 4 slots), whose extra KV + scheduling slowed single-
# stream decode — now auto adds at most ONE extra slot and only with clear VRAM
# headroom after its KV; heavy multi-agent should pin GENESI_TURBO_PARALLEL.
# pkgrel 103: CUDA button productionized via an ISOLATED release-hosted repo.
# The prebuilt genesi-llama-cpp-cuda (122 MB) can't live in the git [genesi] repo
# and must NOT share [genesi-apps] with genesi-code (whose publish rebuilds its
# db from scratch and would clobber it). New dedicated [genesi-cuda] repo (tag
# cuda-repo, db genesi-cuda), published by .github/workflows/publish-llama-cpp-
# cuda.yml (manual/path-triggered; immutable assets like publish-apps). The repo
# is preconfigured in the ISO's pacman.conf(.d)/pacman-target.conf/customize_
# airootfs, and this package's .install seeds it into /etc/pacman.conf on
# existing systems (idempotent). Net effect: the Monitor's "switch to CUDA"
# button + login notifier now install via a plain `pacman -S genesi-llama-cpp-
# cuda` (seconds), no AUR compile, no manual pacman -U.
