# pkgrel 132: Automations flow correctness + visibility (2026-07-19 report:
# "almost all the logic is wrong"). Real bugs found and fixed:
# (1) CONFIG PANEL FIELDS NEVER APPEARED — setNodeConfig/renameNode mutated the
# node in place after a SHALLOW nodes.slice(), so QML saw no identity change
# and never re-evaluated the panel's bindings; the Command sensor's regex input
# only showed up after reselecting the block. Both now build a fresh node
# object (_replaceNode).
# (2) THE WHOLE CANVAS LIT BLUE — the GUI only had a per-automation `running`
# bool. The daemon now publishes PER-NODE state (running/ok/failed/skipped) and
# the canvas colors each card, dimming branches the flow did not take.
# (3) Command sensor is now debuggable: the run log prints the exact text it
# tested plus why it passed/failed (empty regex, invalid regex, exit status).
# Verified headlessly by genesi-arch/ci/automation-engine-test.py (14 asserts:
# ok/error routing, multi-link fan-out from one port, stderr matching, gating,
# sequential order, payload piping).
# pkgrel 133: the Command sensor's gate could be bypassed. A link carrying the
# LEGACY "output" port (the dot existed only in pkgrel 129) fired on "the node
# printed something", ignoring the condition — so a sensor logging
# "did NOT match" still ran the next card (reported 2026-07-19 with a photo).
# Now a condition node that did not pass fires NOTHING, from any port, and
# "output" is treated as a plain default link (GUI renders it as one too).
# Also implements "output changed since last check" for a chained sensor (it
# used to silently behave like exit0) — first run never fires, matching the
# polling trigger's edge semantics. All four Fire-when modes are pinned by
# genesi-arch/ci/automation-engine-test.py (24 asserts).
# pkgrel 135: ANY local .gguf is now a first-class model — no ollama import.
# Until now the Monitor could only run what `ollama pull` had fetched, so using a
# Hugging Face download meant hand-writing a Modelfile in a terminal. That hurt
# most exactly where it mattered: the MoE quants that make a 30B-A3B usable on a
# small card live on HF, and ollama's runner does not expose the expert-offload
# flag (--n-cpu-moe) Turbo already uses to run them fast.
# genesi-ai-turbo's ensure() now resolves a GGUF reference (a path, or
# gguf:<name> from the library) as well as an ollama tag, so EVERY existing Turbo
# feature — MoE expert-offload, Genesi Fit, speculative decoding, autotuned
# profiles — applies to those files unchanged. New subcommands gguf-list /
# gguf-info / gguf-import (JSON out, consumed by the Monitor); library lives in
# ~/.local/share/genesi/models with ~/Downloads and ~/Models also scanned; shard
# sets are collapsed to their first file. pick_draft never guesses a sibling
# draft for a GGUF (a mismatched tokenizer silently corrupts speculation).
# Monitor: the Models page gained a GGUF library card — file picker, drag-and-
# drop, direct-URL download, and per-model badges saying how the file will
# actually run HERE (fits VRAM / MoE offload / slow / CPU) from real VRAM+RAM
# detection. Picking one hands the path to Turbo and switches Turbo on, because
# a raw GGUF can only be served by llama-server (ollama's chat API only knows its
# own registry). Deletion is restricted to files Genesi itself copied in.
# Covered by genesi-arch/ci/gguf-library-test.py (42 asserts) and
# gguf-cli-test.py (29 asserts).
# pkgrel 136: FIX — pkgrel 135 could not open the Monitor at all. AdvisorPage.qml
# bound the ListView scrollbar as a bare `ScrollBar.vertical:`, but this file
# imports QtQuick.Controls AS QQC2, and an attached type must carry its module's
# qualifier. QML treats that as a hard "Non-existent attached object" load error,
# not a warning, so Main.qml could not instantiate AdvisorPage and the whole UI
# died with "Failed to load the Monitor UI (is kirigami installed?)" — the error
# text sent the user hunting a missing dependency that was never the problem
# (reported 2026-07-24, bare metal, Hyprland/caelestia on the RTX 3050).
# One-character class of bug that the Python/CLI tests could never see, because
# it only surfaces when QQmlApplicationEngine actually loads the UI. Fixed to
# `QQC2.ScrollBar.vertical:`, matching every other Genesi QML file, and pinned by
# a NEW genesi-arch/ci/qml-sanity-test.py that checks attached-type qualification
# against each file's imports across all 53 QML files in the repo (verified to
# fail when the bug is reintroduced, and clean on every shipped app).
# pkgrel 137: local GGUFs are now selectable EVERYWHERE, not just on the Models
# page. pkgrel 135 shipped the import but left it an island: the chat picker,
# Quick Chat, the Turbo picker and an automation's AI block all listed only
# `ollama pull` models, so an imported GGUF was invisible to the app that was
# supposed to run it (reported 2026-07-24).
# loadModels() now returns Ollama tags AND local GGUFs in ONE list. A GGUF is
# addressed by the stable string `gguf:<file stem>`, which is why nothing
# downstream needed reshaping — every picker, saved chat session and automation
# node config already stores a model as a string. New modelLabel() renders one
# for humans ("Qwen3 30B A3B · 30B (GGUF)"); pickers show the label but keep the
# raw reference as the value, so choosing a model never rewrites it.
# TRANSPORT is the real fix. Ollama's chat API only knows its own registry, so a
# GGUF can only be served by llama-server — which previously meant it worked only
# with the Turbo switch on. genesi_turbo_ctl.serves_model() now makes a GGUF
# FORCE the llama-server transport and brings the server up on demand, so a local
# model behaves exactly like a pulled one with Turbo on OR off. The switch goes
# back to meaning what it says: speculative decoding and Turbo extras. Applied at
# all four call sites that had a bare `if turbo`: Monitor chat, the agent tool
# loop, Quick Chat (which inherits Backend) and the automation AI block — the
# last one now fails loudly for a GGUF instead of quietly asking Ollama.
# Switching from an auto-started GGUF back to an Ollama tag releases our
# llama-server first, so its VRAM isn't still held while Ollama loads on top; a
# Turbo the USER enabled is never torn down. Quick Chat also falls back to the
# GGUF library when Ollama has no models at all.
# NOTE for future work: Backend gained _prepare_transport, NOT _prepare_model —
# QuickBackend already defines a zero-arg _prepare_model and would have silently
# overridden it, breaking Quick Chat on the first message.
# Covered by genesi-arch/ci/gguf-routing-test.py (37 asserts; stubs PySide6 so
# the real Backend._prepare_transport is exercised headlessly).
# pkgrel 138: a MoE model's parameter count was wildly overstated in every model
# picker — gpt-oss-20b showed as "76.8B". gguf_describe multiplied the GGUF's
# `general.size_label` when it had the "NxM" form, but that notation is not a
# product: experts share the attention and embedding weights, so 32x2.4B is 21B
# (and Mixtral's 8x7B is 46.7B, not 56). Dropped the multiplication and let the
# existing file-size estimate handle those labels — it measures the bytes really
# on disk and lands within a few percent (gpt-oss-20b -> ~21B). Pinned by a new
# case in ci/gguf-library-test.py.
# 2026-08-15 (141): Genesi Mesh awareness. When a model does not fit this
# machine's VRAM but DOES fit across the GPUs of paired machines on the LAN,
# Turbo now passes `--rpc host:port,...` to llama-server so the layers spread
# across them (needs a GGML_RPC=ON build + the genesi-mesh package). Strictly
# additive and fails open in every direction: no genesi-mesh, no daemon, no
# peers, or an llama-server without --rpc, and server_cmd is byte-identical to
# today. Mesh is never used for a model that already fits locally — pooling
# sends activations over the network each token, so it buys "runs at all", not
# speed. Opt out with GENESI_TURBO_NO_MESH=1; force endpoints with
# GENESI_MESH_ENDPOINTS=host:port,...
# 2026-08-15 (142): Genesi AI Assist. A shared gate (genesi_ai_assist.py) that
# every passive, always-on AI helper must ask before touching the model, plus
# the first helper: `genesi-explain`, which puts two dim lines under a command
# that just failed. The gate's rules are the point -- a passive helper NEVER
# loads a model (it rides one already warm on the Turbo daemon or does not run
# at all), stands down while a real inference is running or Studio Mode has the
# machine, is off on battery by default, and caches so the same typo is never
# paid for twice. Fails silent everywhere: no warm model means nothing is
# printed. The shell hook never re-runs the failed command -- it only sees the
# command line and exit code -- because re-running is how a helper deletes
# something twice; pipe `cmd 2>&1 | genesi-explain` when the output matters.
# 2026-08-15 (143): make the Turbo gain visible. `bench` now PERSISTS its
# OFF-vs-ON result to ~/.cache/genesi-ai-mode/bench.json instead of printing a
# number once and losing it, and the Plasma widget shows it ("Turbo: +62%
# medido (21.0 -> 34.0 tok/s)"). Every optimizer in this daemon was invisible
# before -- the user had no way to know what AI Mode bought them. The figure is
# MEASURED on that machine, never estimated, and a negative result is shown as
# a negative result. Until a benchmark exists the widget says so and offers to
# run one.
# 2026-08-15 (144): pass the full GPU probe to Genesi Mesh's planner instead of
# a bare VRAM number, so it can tell a discrete GPU from an integrated one. On a
# laptop with only integrated graphics the shared memory would otherwise look
# like real VRAM, the model would appear to "fit locally", and the mesh would be
# refused in exactly the case it exists for.
# 2026-08-15 (145): the error explainer never fired for most users. The shell
# hook only handled bash and zsh, but fish is Genesi's default interactive
# shell and does not read /etc/profile.d at all, so the feature was inert on the
# very machines most likely to see it. Adds a fish integration installed to
# vendor_conf.d, with the same contract (skip-lists, background, stdin from
# /dev/null, stdout inherited, never re-run the command).
# 2026-08-15 (146): Quick Chat. (1) The summon bar was always tall: the window
# height binding reads body.implicitHeight while body had `anchors.fill:
# parent`, so height flowed both ways and the loop settled at the 620 cap.
# body is now anchored on three sides, making implicitHeight content-driven.
# (2) The agent system prompt was English-only and purely mechanical, which on a
# small local model reads as "takes everything literally". It now says to reply
# in the user's language (most are Brazilian), to read for intent rather than
# literal words given people type fast and abbreviated into a one-line box, to
# stay brief because this is a summon bar, and not to reach for a tool when the
# user only asked a question.
# 2026-08-15 (147): Genesi Mesh page in the AI Mode Monitor, so the feature is
# usable without the terminal: share this GPU, register a machine by address
# (the path that works over a VPN, where multicast discovery cannot), create or
# join a mesh, and run the doctor. Deliberately a thin front-end -- it reads the
# daemon's state.json/peers.json and shells out to `genesi-mesh` via pkexec for
# anything privileged, so the GUI and the CLI cannot drift apart. It also states
# the two things that made a real test fail: an empty peer list is EXPECTED
# across a VPN, and rpc-server has no authentication -- said next to the button.
# 2026-08-15 (148): the error explainer skipped exit code 1, which is what
# pacman, git, cargo, make and nearly every real tool returns when they fail --
# so it stayed silent for almost every case it exists for. A user tested it,
# saw nothing and concluded it was broken. 1 and 2 are no longer skipped; the
# noisy exit-1 commands (grep/diff/test) are handled by the command skip-list,
# which is the right place for them. 126/127 stay skipped because the shell and
# Arch's pkgfile handler already cover 'command not found'.
# 2026-08-15 (149): the error explainer no longer types over the user. It ran
# in the BACKGROUND, so the model's answer (a second or two later) landed after
# the prompt had been drawn -- printing into the middle of whatever was being
# typed, and sometimes explaining a command from a while back. It now runs
# synchronously in the postexec hook, before the next prompt, so the
# explanation appears directly under the failed command like a comment and can
# never interrupt typing. Cost: a short pause after a FAILED command only,
# bounded by `timeout` (now 4s), instant when no model is warm, free on a cache
# hit. Reported from real use.
# 2026-08-16 (150): Turbo says why it did NOT use the mesh. Four different
# situations -- no peers, no --rpc, mesh switched off, model too big even
# pooled -- all returned the same empty tuple and printed nothing, so a user
# who had set up a mesh and was waiting for pooling got an ordinary local
# start with no hint it had been considered. Turbo knew the answer every time
# and never said it. Now it prints one `[mesh] not used: ...` line, but ONLY
# on machines that opted into a mesh (init/join, worker mode, a registered
# peer, or GENESI_MESH_ENDPOINTS) and never when the model simply fits in
# local VRAM -- that is the correct outcome and the common one on exactly the
# GPU machines that have a mesh. Found in a two-machine test.
# 2026-08-16 (151): the Mesh page answers "is it being USED", and a failed
# pooled load no longer kills the server. The page could only show that a mesh
# EXISTED, which is not the question the client machine's user has. It now reads
# the live llama-server's command line (not a marker file, so it stays right
# when a fallback dropped the mesh) and says whether Turbo is pooling and with
# whom, and shows free/total VRAM per machine so a worker with no room left is
# visible instead of silently dropping out of the pool. Turbo also keeps a
# no-mesh variant as the LAST fallback: pooling can fail for a reason this
# machine cannot see (the peer's free VRAM is measured at plan time and consumed
# at load time), and because a pooled command was otherwise "not risky" it went
# straight to os.execvp -- so the load failed with no Turbo left to explain it.
# 2026-08-16 (152): three defects visible in the first real pooled run. The
# [mesh] block printed FOUR times, because server_cmd re-plans on every fallback
# variant it builds and printed from there. Building the no-mesh variant
# announced "[mesh] not used: disabled by GENESI_TURBO_NO_MESH" -- Turbo
# reporting its own scaffolding back as if it were the user's config. And after
# a worker refused the allocation, the next pooled variant asked the same
# machine for the same memory and waited to be refused again; a failure that
# names the mesh now skips straight to the local fallback.
# 2026-08-16 (153): no extra slots over the mesh. The auto-parallel heuristic
# was written for a LOCAL card, where a spare slot costs idle VRAM and nothing
# else. Pooled, it read headroom that lives on ANOTHER machine, put the extra
# slot's KV on the remote GPU, and bought concurrent throughput nobody pooling
# is there for. Measured: a 1.9 GB model with 5.3 GB pooled chose 2 slots, -c
# became 16384, and the load died on the worker; the single-slot retry, identical
# but for that, came up and served fine.
# 2026-08-16 (154): a failed pooled load is no longer silent. Turbo sent the
# server's stdout to /dev/null and llama.cpp splits output across both streams,
# so a crash left a log that stopped at "loading model" and explained nothing --
# which is exactly what a failing mesh load looked like twice in a row. Both
# streams are kept now, the failure line reports HOW the process ended (Popen
# returns a signal as a negative code, so "killed by SIGSEGV" is knowable and
# was being thrown away), and a pooled load that dies mid-way with nothing to
# say now names the likeliest cause: mismatched llama.cpp builds, whose RPC wire
# format is not stable between versions and does not fail politely.
# 2026-08-19 (155): the restart rate limits were never in force. StartLimit*
# belong to [Unit]; genesi-turbo.service and genesi-automationd.service both put
# them in [Service], where systemd ignores them and says so once per unit per
# daemon-reload -- so the ONLY thing those units ever logged was
# "Unknown key 'StartLimitIntervalSec' in section [Service], ignoring", pages of
# it, hiding whatever the service actually had to say. Worse than the noise: a
# Turbo that cannot load its model was meant to give up after 5 tries in 5 min
# and instead retried every RestartSec forever. genesi-studiod.service was fixed
# for exactly this in an earlier release; the other two were missed.
# 2026-08-19 (156): Turbo can serve a machine that has no GPU. The bind address
# and the client URL were both hardcoded to 127.0.0.1, so the ONLY way to use a
# remote GPU was Genesi Mesh -- and Mesh is the wrong tool whenever the model
# fits on the far box, because it ships layer activations for every token
# instead of text. Measured on a 3B, client on WiFi, worker on a 3050: weights
# landed on the GPU fine and the GPU then sat at 2-3% utilisation, idle, waiting
# on a 9 ms round trip with 5 ms of jitter; 1.2 MB/s of tensor traffic bought
# almost no work. GENESI_TURBO_HOST (server) and GENESI_TURBO_URL (clients) now
# override both, defaulting to loopback exactly as before. Turbo has no auth, so
# the docs say plainly this is for a network you control.
# 2026-08-19 (157): and now nobody has to set GENESI_TURBO_URL. Requiring a
# hand-set variable on every GPU-less machine was a bad answer twice over: it is
# a step people forget, and as a fish universal it is invisible to the systemd
# user services that run the passive helpers, so those would have kept talking
# to a loopback Turbo that is not there. Turbo's clients now resolve through the
# mesh -- env override, then a local Turbo, then the peer advertising one, then
# loopback -- and resolve per call, since peers come and go and these processes
# outlive any single answer. Discovery failing can never break inference: every
# path falls back to loopback.
# 2026-08-19 (158): `genesi-ai-turbo share on|off` -- one command instead of a
# remembered ritual. Serving the LAN meant hand-editing turbo.env, opening the
# port under whichever firewall is running, and enabling the unit; three steps,
# each easy to half-do. It is the counterpart to `genesi-mesh worker on` and
# stays an explicit command for the same reason that one is: Turbo has no
# authentication, so leaving loopback is a decision, made once, knowingly. The
# firewall step acts only on an ACTIVE manager and says so when it finds none --
# a rule written to a stopped ufw reports success and filters nothing.
# 2026-08-19 (159): `share` is documented as the MECHANISM, not the command to
# reach for. Sharing compute across machines is Genesi Mesh's job and belongs
# next to `genesi-mesh worker`; this stays because turbo.env, the unit and the
# port are ai-mode's to own, and Mesh delegates rather than reaching into
# another package's files. `genesi-mesh serve on|off` is what users run.
# 2026-08-19 (160): `serve` on a GPU-less machine defers to a peer already
# serving Turbo. Discovery (157) only rerouted the CLIENTS -- the passive
# helpers and the Monitor -- so `genesi-ai-turbo serve llama3.2:3b`, the one
# command a user actually types, still built a local CPU server while a real
# GPU sat idle one hop away. It now says what it found and uses the peer;
# --local (or GENESI_TURBO_LOCAL=1) forces a local server, and a machine WITH a
# GPU is never redirected, since there `serve` means "serve from here".
# Also fixes a bug (157) introduced by that same discovery: turbo_healthy()
# resolved through turbo_url(), so while a local load was still in progress it
# would ask a PEER whether Turbo was up -- a failed local start could report
# success on the strength of another machine answering. It now checks loopback
# unless given an explicit URL.
# 2026-08-19 (161): genesi-turbo.service could never pull a model, and picked
# the wrong one to pull. It ran with no HOME, and `ollama` does not degrade
# without it -- it PANICS ("panic: $HOME is not defined", out of
# envconfig.Models(), before the CLI is even constructed). So any warm model not
# already on disk failed at the pull, the unit exited 1, and the journal carried
# a Go stack trace instead of a cause. StateDirectory + Environment=HOME fixes
# that and gives the optional slot cache somewhere to live under ProtectHome.
# The model it tried to pull was wrong too: ExecStart used `recommend`, which
# answers "the biggest model this GPU COULD run" -- advice, not a boot decision
# -- so a box with a 3B downloaded started up pulling an unrequested 8B. New
# `recommend --installed` restricts to what is on disk and prints nothing when
# nothing fits, letting the existing fallback handle a bare machine.
# 2026-08-19 (162): `share on` was fighting genesi-aid and losing. aid reads
# always_warm_turbo every poll and STOPS genesi-turbo.service whenever the knob
# is off, so enabling the unit -- which is what `share on` (and advanced.conf's
# own advice) did -- produced a server that came up, served for about a second,
# and was stopped. The journal said "Deactivated successfully", which reads as a
# clean shutdown, not as another daemon undoing you. `share` now sets the knob
# in the same operation, and advanced.conf no longer tells people to enable the
# unit directly.
# 2026-08-19 (163): the Monitor gets the choice, and stops assuming loopback.
# TURBO was a hardcoded 127.0.0.1 constant, which made the Monitor the last
# component still insisting inference happens on this machine; it resolves
# through the mesh now. New turboSources() slot feeds a "Onde a IA roda" card on
# the Mesh page listing every Turbo available (this machine and each serving
# peer, each with the model it is running) and letting the user pick, plus a
# Servir Turbo button beside the existing Compartilhar GPU one. `serve` and
# `use` added to meshAdmin's whitelist -- both are mesh subcommands needing
# root, which is what that pkexec bridge exists for. `serve` also records the
# model tag to /run/genesi-ai-mode/turbo-model, since llama.cpp's /props reports
# the GGUF blob path and under ollama that is a sha256 filename.
# 2026-08-19 (164): the Monitor's Turbo toggle still started a LOCAL server even
# when the Mesh page was, correctly, showing that Turbo lived on another
# machine. _start_turbo() spawned `genesi-ai-turbo serve` unconditionally, so a
# GPU-less client reported the peer in the UI and sent the work to its own CPU,
# where it sat at "loading model" on a model far too big for it. It now checks
# for a healthy peer first and starts nothing; the off path no longer kills a
# local warm server it never started. Also splits the endpoint in two, the same
# distinction (162) fixed inside genesi-ai-turbo: LOCAL_TURBO for "is the server
# WE started alive", _turbo_base() for "where should inference go" -- one
# constant for both means a peer's /health can answer for our own failed load.
# And the Mesh page's new card had no height: an FCard is a plain Rectangle, so
# `anchors.fill` collapsed the layout to zero while its children kept painting,
# drawing the card on top of "Máquinas". It follows the page's own convention
# now (implicitHeight bound to its column).
# 2026-08-19 (165): the "does this machine have a GPU" test that gates the
# deferral was measured with _detect_vram_mb(), which falls back to what VULKAN
# reports -- and inside a VM the software rasteriser answers with a slice of
# system RAM. So a machine with no GPU at all measured GBs of "VRAM", the gate
# said "you have a GPU, serve locally", and a client sat loading a model on its
# CPU with a ready GPU one hop away. It now asks genesi_mesh_common.probe_gpu(),
# the same number `genesi-mesh plan` prints, so the two finally agree. An
# explicit `genesi-mesh use <peer>` pin also defers regardless of local
# hardware -- the user said where they wanted it. And when a peer IS serving but
# we serve locally anyway, `serve` now SAYS so and names the command to switch:
# the failure mode was silence, which is why this took a round trip to find.
# 2026-08-19 (166): `share on` opened 11435 in the DEFAULT zone only. A mesh
# normally has a LAN NIC and a VPN up at once, in different firewalld zones, and
# clients are handed whichever address the beacon arrived from -- so on a real
# pair the port was open on `public` (the cable) and shut on `mesh`
# (tailscale0), the client got the Tailscale address, and /health timed out.
# It reads like a dead service, not like a firewall, and nothing in any output
# mentioned either. Now opens/closes on every zone peer_facing_zones() reports.
# `serve` also says when the Turbo it was told to use does not answer, instead
# of falling through to the pooling path whose unrelated "no worker peers
# online" was the only line on screen.
# 2026-08-19 (167): you could not see what was running, so you could not stop
# it. Ollama holds a model for OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE (15m as Genesi ships it) after
# the LAST request, so one closed chat left a 2.3 GB runner pinned to the CPU --
# defensible behaviour, and invisible: Turbo has a unit and a port, Ollama has a
# timer, and nothing ever put the two on one screen. A user found theirs by
# suspecting it and running `ollama ps`. New `genesi-ai-turbo status` (Turbo,
# every Ollama-resident model with size/processor/expiry, and whether inference
# is actually happening on a mesh peer) and `genesi-ai-turbo stop` (unload it
# all). `serve off` now lists what it did NOT stop -- it ends the server, never
# Ollama's keep-alive, and to whoever typed "stop serving" both are "the AI".
# The Monitor grows a "Rodando nesta máquina agora" card with a Descarregar
# button, so this is answerable without a terminal.
# 2026-08-20 (168): two new AI Assist helpers.
# (1) THE FIX IS NOW ONE KEYPRESS. The explainer already printed the command
# that would fix the failure and then made the user retype it. That command is
# now offered as dim ghost text at the next prompt, accepted with →. zsh gets
# real inline ghost text (POSTDISPLAY, only ever on an empty line, so it cannot
# fight zsh-autosuggestions); fish -- Genesi's default shell -- has no API for
# setting an autosuggestion (fish-shell#9809), so the fix goes into fish's
# history and fish draws the ghost itself as you type (`suggest_fix_history`
# turns that off); bash cannot draw ghost text at all, so it gets the → binding
# plus a printed marker. Nothing is ever executed for the user, → only types it,
# and fixes that delete or overwrite (rm, dd, mkfs, git reset --hard, curl | sh)
# are printed but NEVER armed on a keypress. In every shell the key is borrowed
# only while a fix is pending and handed straight back afterwards.
# (2) genesi-find: describe a file instead of naming it, in English or
# Portuguese, from the terminal or from Dolphin's right-click menu ("Search with
# AI here"). The model NEVER touches the disk -- it only turns the sentence into
# a filter (words, extensions, a date range), which is validated and then run
# locally by a plain directory walk; anything the model returns that is not a
# word, an extension, a small integer or a known folder name is dropped. With no
# model warm it still searches, using the built-in bilingual parser. The gate
# gained a `passive=False` path for helpers the user INVOKED: those skip the
# battery/Studio/busy courtesy checks, because the user is sitting there waiting
# -- but rule 1 is unchanged, nothing here ever loads a model.
# 2026-08-20 (169): the Automations tab grows the pieces a real workflow needs.
# (1) VALUES BETWEEN BLOCKS. An AI block can declare named outputs; the schema
# goes into the prompt and the reply is validated coming back, so a notification
# can say "CPU {{cpu}}%, GPU {{gpu}}%" instead of pasting prose at the user. A
# reply missing a declared field FAILS the block rather than rendering half a
# result; the old {input} (the previous output as one lump) still works.
# (2) CONDITION node with True/False ports, decided by an expression
# ({{cpu}} > 80) or by asking the local model a yes/no question. A false answer
# paints GREEN -- false is an answer, not a failure, and red would teach people
# that a working graph looks broken. No eval() anywhere: the expression comes
# from a JSON file on disk. Ordering comparisons demand numbers on both sides,
# because "quite high" > "80" is TRUE in ASCII and would have fired the
# automation on an answer nobody could measure.
# (3) LOOP (for each / after) and SUB-WORKFLOW. The loop body is walked
# synchronously: the walk is breadth-first over one shared variable dict, so
# queueing the body per item would run every iteration after the loop finished
# and each would read the LAST item. Both share one step budget and stop at
# three levels of nesting; a workflow cannot call itself.
# (4) New triggers: clipboard, screenshot, webhook, and cron on the schedule
# node. The webhook binds loopback unless a token is set -- it runs shell
# commands, and an open port with no secret is a shell for the whole LAN.
# (5) EMAIL (IMAP read / SMTP send). The password is NEVER in the workflow: the
# node stores an account name and the secret lives in the session keyring, read
# via secret-tool at send time. Workflow files get copied, synced and pasted
# into forum posts when asking for help.
# (6) "Build with AI": describe an automation and have it drawn. Every kind and
# every field is in the prompt, anything invented is dropped AND named back to
# the user, and when it cannot build what was asked it says so and offers the
# nearest thing it can. The catalogue + validator live in the Qt-free
# genesi_workflow_gen.py so they are testable without PySide6.
# Covered by ci/automation-engine-test.py (94 checks) and
# ci/workflow-generator-test.py (33).
# 2026-08-20 (170): the schedule node gets a VISUAL cron builder — pickers that
# write the expression and a sentence underneath that reads it back in plain
# language ("At 09:00 on weekdays."), so the check is "does that describe what I
# wanted" instead of "did I count the asterisks". The raw field stays and stays
# authoritative: anything the pickers cannot express can still be typed, and
# they stop claiming to describe it rather than guessing. An expression Genesi
# cannot parse says so, because a schedule that silently never fires is the
# worst outcome here. Also: links now animate while a run is in flight, and only
# while it is — an idle canvas repaints zero times, which matters on a machine
# whose selling point is having CPU left for the model.
# 2026-08-20 (171): EVERY block now hands named values to the blocks after it.
# Flow of control was never the missing half — a link already said what runs
# next. Flow of DATA was: the only channel was {input}, the previous block's
# whole output as one string, so a graph could sequence three cards correctly
# and still have no way to take the number out of one and put it in another.
# Reported from real use, with clipboard -> AI -> notification on screen.
# Every kind publishes its own values now (script.stdout/stderr/exit,
# http.status/body, clipboard.text, file.path, resource.value, mail.subject,
# cond.answer, ai.reply, the AI block's declared fields, …), each landing twice:
# namespaced ({{script.exit}}) and bare ({{exit}}) for the one-obvious-source
# case, with the most recent block along the path winning the bare name.
# One catalogue (NODE_OUTPUTS in genesi_workflow_gen.py) feeds all three places
# that would otherwise drift: the daemon publishes against it, the config panel
# shows it, and the generator's prompt carries it so a generated graph wires
# names that exist instead of names a model imagined.
# Discoverability was the other half of the report: every block's panel now
# lists the values reaching it — walked BACKWARDS through the links, so it only
# ever offers values from blocks that have actually run — click to copy, plus a
# line saying what this block provides in turn.
# Fixed on the way: the power trigger referenced a threshold variable that only
# exists in another branch (NameError at fire time on AC/battery events).
# 2026-08-21 (172): the Turbo switch showed INTENT, not reality. Anything can
# start Turbo -- an Automation, the CLI, a Mesh peer, a server left running from
# the last session -- and the switch only ever reflected what this window had
# last asked for. So the machine could be serving a model with the switch
# sitting at off, which is the UI telling the user something untrue about their
# own computer (reported from real use, via an automation that enables Turbo).
# state() now carries turbo_running/turbo_model from the same probe the rest of
# the stack uses, and the 2s poll adopts it -- including the model actually
# being served, so a running server is not labelled with whatever this window
# happened to have picked. Two guards are load-bearing: `turbo_busy` suppresses
# the sync while a start we asked for is still coming up (a start takes seconds
# to answer /health, and syncing then would flick the switch back under the
# user's finger), and `turboSyncing` makes the property handlers stand down
# during the copy -- without it the sync is a feedback loop, where the poll
# assigns turboRequested, the handler fires, and setTurbo restarts the very
# server it was only trying to display.
# 2026-08-21 (173): three things reported from one real automation ("when
# Firefox opens ask the AI for its RAM, when it closes ask again, notify with
# both"), plus a crash that had been shipping for weeks.
# (1) QUICK CHAT WAS DEAD ON ARRIVAL. genesi_ai_quick imported TURBO from
# genesi_ai_monitor; the Mesh work renamed that to LOCAL_TURBO and did not
# update the importer, so an app that autostarts on every login died on its
# import line, silently, since c114c95c. It now asks _turbo_base() where Turbo
# is, which is what the rename was for. New ci/module-imports-test.py checks
# every cross-module import by AST -- no Qt, no display -- because an
# autostarted app that fails leaves no window to miss.
# (2) AN APP BLOCK MID-CHAIN NOW WAITS. Reached through a link it used to pass
# straight through, so "App opens -> AI -> App closes -> Notify" ran the whole
# graph the instant the app opened and the second half measured nothing. It
# blocks until the app is in the state it names, and a timeout is an ERROR
# rather than a silent continue, so the err port can handle it.
# (3) "ANY APP" instead of "never". An empty process name meant the node could
# never fire, while looking perfectly configured. It now fires for whatever
# opens or closes and reports it as {{app.name}}; a burst fires once.
# (4) OUTPUT NAMES ARE VALIDATED. Someone declared "RAM {{app.name}} usage" as
# a field name -- something {{ }} can never reference. The model was asked for
# it, helpfully substituted the placeholder, and the block failed with
# "expected JSON with ..." three steps from the mistake. The editor now slugs
# the name as it is typed and says it did; the daemon refuses the rest and names
# a spelling that would work.
# 2026-08-21 (174): "any app closed" ran the workflow forever, and it was my
# own doing. Running a workflow STARTS processes -- the shell behind Run Script,
# notify-send, a model server, python -- and when they exit a trigger watching
# for any app closing sees them and starts the workflow again, which starts more
# of them. The loop is real, it is fast, and nothing in the user's graph is
# wrong. A trigger now stands down while its own automation is still running,
# and for five seconds afterwards, which is when the last run's children are
# being reaped. Nothing legitimate is lost: the pollers re-check every couple of
# seconds, so an event that still matters is still there. Covered by five checks
# in ci/automation-engine-test.py.
# 2026-08-21 (175): two reports that turned out to be the same story told from
# both ends -- what you typed was not what the block ran.
# (1) FIELDS NOW SAVE WHILE YOU TYPE. They only saved on Enter or focus loss,
# and clicking from a field straight onto ANOTHER CARD swapped the panel out
# before editingFinished ever arrived. The text was gone and the block kept
# running with its previous value, which is how an output name nobody had typed
# in a while kept appearing in the error messages. Debounced at 350ms -- a pause
# in typing, not a keystroke -- and guarded on activeFocus, because `text` is
# bound to the config and an unguarded save would rewrite the binding that fired
# it.
# (2) THE MODEL SPELLS THE KEY ITS OWN WAY. Asked for {"ram_before": ...} a
# small local model answers {"RAM before": ...} or {"RAM llama-server usage":
# ...} -- shape right, spelling its own -- and an exact-key match failed on
# replies that plainly contained the number requested. Keys are now compared
# with case, spaces and punctuation folded away, and a single requested field
# facing a single returned value is taken whatever it was called. With SEVERAL
# fields it still refuses: guessing which value belongs in which slot is how a
# workflow silently reports the GPU figure as the CPU one.
# 2026-08-26 (176): "Run now" could not pass a value along. A graph with no
# trigger block on the sheet -- AI Action -> Notification, hit Run now -- went
# down a SECOND runner: a flat topological sweep that called each action with
# payload="" and no vars dict. With vars=None every _publish() is a no-op, so
# the AI block's {{ai.reply}} and every custom output it declared were never
# published, and the notification rendered them as the literal braces the user
# typed; {input} arrived empty for the same reason. The links were not read
# either, so on-ok/on-error did nothing. That path now builds a real _Walk and
# enters it at the cards nothing links into, which means one runner, one set of
# rules, whether or not there is a trigger on the sheet. Unconnected cards on a
# sheet still all run -- that is what the old sweep was for. Six checks in
# ci/automation-engine-test.py, including the reported graph end to end.
# 2026-08-26 (177): "put an App block in front of the AI and the outputs start
# failing with expected JSON; take it away and they work." The App block was
# never the cause. In autonomous / ask mode the AGENT protocol owns the reply
# format: the moment the model uses a tool, the run ends in a sentence about
# what it found -- "Descobri que seu sistema usa uma Intel UHD Graphics 620" --
# which is the right answer wearing the wrong clothes, and the declared outputs
# were matched against it and failed. Whether that happened at all came down to
# whether the prompt tempted the model into a tool call, and the trigger detail
# that rides along in the prompt ("app opened: firefox") is exactly such a
# nudge -- so the bug looked like it belonged to the block in front. The AI
# block now spends ONE repair turn: plain chat, no agent prompt in the way,
# shown only the original question and its own answer, asked to write that same
# answer as the object it declared. Nothing is invented and a reformat that
# fails still fails the block. When it does fail from a non-advisory block the
# log now names the trap instead of leaving it to be deduced. Nine checks in
# ci/automation-engine-test.py.
# 2026-08-26 (178): five things reported from one afternoon of real use.
# (1) THE ENABLED SWITCH STOPPED FOLLOWING THE SELECTION. GToggle flipped its
# own `checked` on click, which DESTROYS the binding the call site had written
# (checked: root.activeEnabled). From the first click onward, selecting a
# disabled automation left the label reading "Disabled" beside a switch still
# sitting in the on position. It now only reports the click; the owner turns it.
# (2) THE RUN LOG FOUGHT BACK. It jumped to the bottom on every new line, so
# scrolling up to read something during a run was impossible, and there was no
# way to empty it. It follows the tail only while the user IS at the tail, gains
# a Clear button, and appends new lines instead of rebuilding the whole list
# every 1.5s (which was also throwing away any selection in progress).
# (3) TEXT WAS NOT SELECTABLE. An answer you cannot copy out of the window is
# half an answer. Chat bubbles and run-log lines are read-only TextEdits now.
# (4) TWO APP BLOCKS OVERWROTE EACH OTHER. "Firefox opens -> ask the AI for the
# RAM -> Firefox closes -> ask again" has two App cards, both publishing
# app.name, so the back half of the graph reported whichever ran last -- and the
# panel's value list silently dropped the second card's entry as a duplicate. A
# block can now carry a value name and publishes under that too
# ({{opened.name}}), which nothing else can take. The shared {{app.name}} keeps
# its last-wins meaning for every graph already saved.
# (5) "ANY APP" MEANT ANY PROCESS. A shell, a python helper, a thumbnailer --
# something starts or exits every few seconds on a live desktop, so an empty app
# name fired more or less constantly while looking like it was waiting for the
# user to open something. It now matches against what the installed .desktop
# files actually launch; if none can be read it fires for anything, because a
# node that never fires is worse than one that fires too often.
# Twenty-two checks in ci/automation-engine-test.py.
# 2026-08-26 (179): "{{app1.name}} in the second App card does not find the
# app." It never could: _wait_app read cfg["app"] straight instead of rendering
# it, so the block waited for a process literally named "{{app1.name}}" until it
# timed out. It was not alone -- launch/close app, the file operations, the
# sound path and the wait duration all skipped _sub as well, which meant no
# value from anywhere in the run could reach ANY of them. All five render now.
# (The App POLLER stays raw on purpose: it is the trigger, and it runs before
# there is a run to read values from.)
# Looking for others turned up three fields the generator advertised that
# nothing reads: act_file said "source" where the panel and daemon say "src",
# and act_app / act_power said "action" where both say "op". The validator drops
# any key not in the catalogue, so a generated block carried the wrong name or
# no name at all and then sat on the canvas looking configured while copying no
# files and launching no apps. A new check in ci/workflow-generator-test.py
# reads the daemon with ast and fails if the catalogue ever again names a field
# no executor reads. Eight more checks in ci/automation-engine-test.py.
# 2026-08-26 (180): swept the whole feature for the classes of bug the last
# three releases kept finding one at a time, instead of waiting for the next
# report. Three files have to spell every field name identically -- the panel
# writes it, the daemon reads it, the catalogue tells the model about it -- and
# every disagreement shows up as "the block looks configured and does nothing".
# FOUR BLOCKS ADVERTISED VALUES THEY NEVER PUBLISHED. The panel offered
# {{process.name}}, {{process.metric}}, {{process.value}}, {{command.output}},
# {{file.dest}} and {{app.name}}; nothing ever created them, so writing one into
# the next block rendered it as its own braces. evt_process, evt_command (as a
# trigger AND mid-chain), act_file and act_app now publish what they promise,
# and the file/app blocks hand their result on as the payload too.
# TWO MORE FIELD-NAME MISMATCHES, found by reading the daemon rather than by
# use: evt_command said "pattern" where the panel and daemon say "match" (so a
# generated sensor set to "output matches" matched an empty regex) and had no
# "interval" at all.
# THE VALUE NAME IS NO LONGER APP-ONLY. Two App cards fighting over
# {{app.name}} was the report; two Run Scripts over {{stdout}} is the same bug
# with a different noun. Every executor that publishes now passes its block's
# own prefix, and the panel shows the field on any block that publishes
# anything.
# All three invariants are now pinned by ci/workflow-generator-test.py, which
# parses the daemon with ast and fails if the catalogue or the panel ever again
# names a field no executor reads, or advertises a value no executor publishes.
# Verified by reintroducing the three fixed bugs and watching it fail. 200
# checks in ci/automation-engine-test.py, 132 in the generator suite.
# 2026-08-26 (181): the Monitor UI, rebuilt around the mockup -- and a harness
# that made it possible to SEE what was being built. devtools/preview.py plus a
# nine-type Kirigami stub run the app on a machine that is not Genesi, so this
# pass was rendered and looked at rather than written blind (which is how
# pkgrel 135 shipped a blank Monitor).
# THE CHAT IS A DIFFERENT PAGE. The empty state was an icon and two lines
# saying the chat was empty, which the empty chat had already conveyed. It is
# now a hero with three suggestion cards that say what the thing can DO and
# fill the composer when clicked, over a row of pills reaching the rest of the
# app. The composer holds the message and everything that applies to it --
# model, Genesi Find, reload -- instead of the model picker sitting in the top
# bar two thirds of a window from the text it governs. Enter sends,
# Shift+Enter breaks the line, and the box grows to five lines.
# GENESI FIND IS IN THE CHAT. Turn the pill on and the next message searches
# your FILES instead of going to the model, through `genesi-find --json` -- the
# same single source of truth as genesi-find-ui and the Dolphin action, so the
# chat cannot drift into a second, weaker search. Results arrive as CARDS:
# type, name, folder, age, size. Clicking one opens it; "show in folder"
# reveals it SELECTED via org.freedesktop.FileManager1.ShowItems, which is the
# thing "open the containing folder" never does.
# IT IS ACTUALLY RESPONSIVE NOW. Two breakpoints: the right rail leaves below
# 1180 (everything on it is reachable elsewhere) and the sidebar collapses to
# an icon rail below 1000 -- collapses rather than disappears, because losing
# navigation would strand the user on whatever page they were on. The window
# minimum was 936px, WIDER than the point the layout collapses at, so the
# collapsed layout could never be reached; the floor is now 684. Two layout
# bugs fell out of testing at those sizes: the sidebar history was an unbounded
# Repeater that pushed the Memory Palace card off the bottom of the window (it
# scrolls now), and the nav items set `height` where a ColumnLayout wants
# Layout.preferredHeight, so they stretched to fill the rail.
# The shared Theme.qml gains a radius/spacing/type scale so the apps stop each
# inventing their own. Additive -- nothing else changes.
# 2026-08-26 (182): first pass over the Dashboard, and the card chrome the
# whole fleet shares.
# THE METRIC ROW REFLOWS. Three 128px cards side by side stop being readable
# long before the window stops shrinking -- the gauge and its number end up
# sharing about ninety pixels. Two columns under 1180, one under 820.
# THE CARD CHROME IS CALMER. GlassCard's radius was 18 while everything around
# it used 18/14/12/10, so two cards side by side rounded differently; it is now
# 16, matching the kit's one card radius. And an ACTIVE card drew the raw
# accent at full saturation plus a 0.28 ring plus a 0.50 halo -- on the Turbo
# card that read as an alarm rather than "this is on", and it was the loudest
# thing in the window, competing with the numbers that are the actual content.
# 0.30 / 0.12 / 0.22 now. This is the shared GlassCard, so every app using it
# gets the same calm.
# THE SHOUTED MICRO-HEADINGS. Five ALL-CAPS letterspaced accent labels (CPU,
# MEMORY, INFERENCE...) competed with the readings underneath. Same size,
# normal tracking, secondary colour -- the icon chip beside each already
# carries the colour.
# Still to do on this app: the Models, Automations and Mesh pages, and
# QuickChat.
# 2026-08-26 (183): the Models page, and a bug the preview harness found on a
# page I was not even working on.
# THE ADVISOR'S FAILURES WERE ITS ANSWER. advise() returned the raw OSError
# text on any exception, and the page rendered it in the same monospace box the
# advice goes in -- with wrapMode NoWrap, so it ran off the right edge. A
# machine without the genesi-ai-mode CLI therefore showed "error querying the
# advisor: [Errno 2] No such file or directory" as its hardware advice. Absent
# and broken are now different things: absent returns "" and gets the page's
# empty state, a real failure gets a framed error row with a retry, and the
# advice itself wraps.
# THE MODELS PAGE STOPPED BORROWING TURBO'S ORANGE. Orange means Turbo in this
# app; on a page whose nav item is green it was doing duty as "local model
# file", and it was the loudest thing on screen. The genuinely Turbo-related
# bits (the ACTIVE model, the MoE fit badge) keep it.
# AND: CanvasTheme.qml never defined `violet`, which AutomationCanvas.qml and
# AutomationConfigPanel.qml both use -- so two icons on the Automations page
# were handed `undefined` as a colour and drew nothing. Invisible on a dark
# background; the only trace was a QML warning nobody sees without running the
# app, which is exactly what devtools/preview.py now makes possible. Fixed, and
# ci/qml-sanity-test.py fails if that family ever again uses a token its own
# theme does not define (verified by removing violet and watching it fail).
# 2026-08-26 (184): the Mesh page, and three more bugs that only a running app
# could show.
# "NOT INSTALLED" STOPPED BEING AN ERROR. The Mesh page announced a missing
# optional package in a red box with a red border -- the same thing the app
# says when something has gone wrong. Painting "you have not installed this
# yet" red is how people learn to ignore red. It is an invitation now: what it
# is, why you would want it, and the pacman command with a copy button.
# UNDEFINED WAS BEING ASSIGNED TO bool. `a && a.b` evaluates to UNDEFINED, not
# false, when `a` is missing, and QML cannot coerce that: it warns and KEEPS
# THE PREVIOUS VALUE. Four `visible:` bindings on the Mesh page were written
# that way, so a row could stay on screen describing state that was no longer
# there. Coerced with !!.
# THE PALETTE DRAG GHOST NEVER WORKED. `PaletteItem { dragGhost: dragGhost }`
# binds the property to ITSELF -- inside the delegate the component's own
# property shadows the outer id -- so Qt detected a binding loop, broke it, and
# every palette item was left holding null. Dragging a block from the palette
# showed no ghost. The ghost item is `paletteGhost` now.
# And the canvas's undo Shortcut used the singular `sequence`, which binds only
# the first of the chords StandardKey.Undo maps to.
# The Mesh subtitle was hardcoded pt-BR while the rest of the app switches
# language from the top bar, so an English user read that page in Portuguese.
# Every tab now renders with ZERO QML warnings.
# 2026-08-26 (185): the Automations canvas, against the reference sheet.
# EVERY CARD SAYS WHAT KIND OF STEP IT IS. A coloured strip across the top
# carries the category (TRIGGER / AI / CONDITION / ACTION / LOOP) and the step
# number. On a sheet of a dozen cards that is the difference between scanning
# and deciphering: the title says what THIS one does, the strip says whether it
# is a trigger, a decision, or something that touches the machine. The link
# animation is untouched.
# THE INSPECTOR STOPPED SPILLING OUT OF ITS OWN PANEL. FieldLabel had neither
# fillWidth nor wrapping, so one long label ("Process name — leave empty for
# ANY app") set the column's implicit width and pushed every field below it
# past the panel's right edge, where the window clipped them.
# ZOOM IS ONE BAR THAT SAYS WHAT THE ZOOM IS. Three 34px squares stacked in a
# corner read as three unrelated buttons, and none of them answered the
# question you actually have when a graph looks wrong: how far in am I. Now
# "− + 100% Fit", with the mini-map sitting above it.
# CanvasTheme gains the shape/rhythm/type scale, mirroring the shared Theme
# token for token. The canvas keeps its own PALETTE on purpose; it should not
# also invent its own geometry. The sanity check caught the first attempt at
# this within seconds of writing it.
# 2026-08-26 (186): Quick Chat -- the Hyprland sizing bug, and the overlay
# itself.
# IT CAME UP THE FULL HEIGHT OF THE SCREEN, and went visibly wrong the moment
# you asked it something. Two causes, both about who decides the size. The
# window declared a `height:` binding and nothing else, which is a request the
# client makes to ITSELF -- it never told the compositor a maximum, so a
# compositor free to choose a height chose one. And the card inside used
# anchors.fill, so it was exactly as tall as whatever surface arrived: content
# in the top fifth of a huge empty panel. The window now declares
# minimumHeight/maximumHeight (Qt forwards these as the xdg_toplevel min/max
# size), and the card is sized by its CONTENT, so the panel is the right shape
# even where the constraint is ignored and the surplus surface is simply
# transparent. Verified by forcing a 700px window in the preview: the card
# stays 92px instead of stretching.
# THE OVERLAY SPEAKS THE APP'S LANGUAGE NOW. Same rounded surface and hairline
# as the chat composer it is a miniature of. "ASK" was a 34x26 badge with 9px
# text -- a label pretending to be a button, and the thing you press most in
# this window; it is a real button with a state. And the settings/close
# controls used freedesktop icon NAMES, which resolve against whatever icon
# theme the desktop happens to have -- on a system missing those two they were
# empty squares. They use the app's own bundled SVGs, like everything else in
# the window.
# devtools/preview.py runs Quick Chat too, and picks up an app's Backend
# SUBCLASS rather than the base class it imports.
# `theme: theme` binds the property TO ITSELF -- QML resolves a bare name on
# the right-hand side against the object's own properties before the enclosing
# scope's ids -- so every GButton/StudioCard/StatusBanner written that way was
# handed `undefined` and rendered unstyled. Swept here too; the id is
# `appTheme` now. ci/qml-sanity-test.py fails on the pattern from now on.
# (AdvisorPage and the genesi-find-ui window were both affected.)
# 2026-08-26 (188): genesi-find-ui shows the same card the chat does.
# Genesi Find answers in two places, and a file looked like one thing in the
# chat and another in the standalone window: there it was a flat 58px row that
# opened only on a DOUBLE tap, with nothing on screen saying it was clickable
# and no way to reveal it in a file manager -- which is usually what you want
# after finding something. It is FileCard now, bundled into find/ at build
# alongside FIcon and the icon set, so both surfaces get the type badge, the
# single-click open, "show in folder" and "copy path" from one component. The
# find backend gained a copyToClipboard alias, because one component driving
# two backends means the two backends answer to the same verb.
# Its empty state kept the three-state logic it already had (searching / found
# nothing / not asked yet) and gained a shape. I nearly added a SECOND empty
# state next to it before noticing -- the same duplication this pass has been
# taking out of the other apps.
# 2026-08-26 (189): the Dashboard now reads like the chat.
# THE ANSWER IS IN THE MIDDLE OF THE WINDOW. The status was a wide banner: a
# ring on the left, four stacked lines in the middle, and an ON/OFF pill
# floating at the far right -- you read it left to right to find out the one
# thing you opened the Dashboard for. It is the chat's hero shape now: the orb,
# the state at headline size, one line qualifying it, and the rest as pills
# underneath (tweaks, Turbo and its model, the governor). The orb only breathes
# while the machine is actually generating; a pulse that never stops stops
# meaning anything.
# THE METRIC CARDS LOST THEIR DONUTS. Each led with a large grey ring holding
# an em dash whenever there was no reading yet, which is the emptiest possible
# way to say "nothing here". Same card language as the chat's suggestions now:
# tinted icon chip, name, the number at headline size, a slim bar that simply
# is not drawn when there is nothing to measure, and one line of context.
# THE TURBO CARD STOPPED SHOUTING. As a GlassCard with accent: turbo and
# active: on it drew a saturated border, ring and halo around the widest thing
# on the page -- beside the calm cards under it, the one element shouting was
# the one already saying it was on three other ways. Same surface as its
# neighbours; the orange stays in the icon, the badge and the switch.
pkgrel=192
pkgdesc='Genesi OS AI Mode - adaptive, reversible local-AI optimizer (CPU/GPU/power) + Plasma 6 widget + monitor app'
arch=('any')
url='https://github.com/Genesi-OS/GenesiOS'
license=('AGPL-3.0-or-later')
# pyside6 + qqc2-desktop-style + kirigami are pulled in as hard deps so the AI
# Mode Monitor (genesi-ai-monitor) — a standalone Qt6/Kirigami app
# (`import org.kde.kirigami`) — works out of the box, dark-themed, wherever
# genesi-ai-mode is installed. The live ISO and the target both list
# genesi-ai-mode, so pacman resolves these automatically (no need to edit package
# lists / the Calamares submodule). kirigami is depended on DIRECTLY (not via
# plasma-workspace) so a Hyprland-only install doesn't get the whole Plasma shell
# — see the pkgrel 73 note above.
# genesi-llama-cpp (Vulkan llama.cpp, provides=llama.cpp) ships `llama-server`,
# the backend of Genesi Turbo's ⚡ switch. It is shipped by default via the
# install lists (archiso packages_desktop.x86_64 + the Calamares netinstall
# "Genesi OS" group), NOT as a hard dep here: it's built by the SAME publish
# pipeline, so a `depends=genesi-llama-cpp` would make the build-deps step
# `pacman -S` a package that isn't in any repo yet (and makepkg's dep check
# would also fail) → aborting the whole publish. It's an optdepend for docs.
# It conflicts with the AUR llama.cpp variants, so those are no longer listed.
depends=('python' 'python-psutil' 'kirigami' 'pyside6' 'qqc2-desktop-style' 'qt6-svg'
         # genesi-ai-tray (standalone SNI tray applet) — libappindicator/Gtk, NOT
         # Qt's QSystemTrayIcon (broken with the caelestia/Quickshell bar on Qt 6.11)
         'python-gobject' 'libayatana-appindicator' 'gtk3')
optdepends=(
    'plasma-workspace: AI Mode Plasma 6 desktop widget (KDE/Plasma session only; pulled automatically by the KDE-Desktop install)'
    'power-profiles-daemon: switch to the performance power profile while AI runs'
    'nvidia-utils: NVIDIA GPU performance mode (persistence + max power limit)'
    'ollama: run local models (genesi-ai-mode bench)'
    'qt6-tools: qdbus for the KWin desktop-effect trimmer'
    'genesi-llama-cpp: Genesi Turbo speculative decoding (llama-server, Vulkan — shipped by default)'
    'genesi-ttf-rubik-vf: Rubik brand UI font for the Monitor (shipped by default; falls back to the system sans if absent)'
    'libnotify: desktop notifications from Automations (notify-send)'
)
install="$pkgname.install"
# Opt-in advanced tweaks config (all OFF by default). backup() so pacman writes
# .pacnew instead of clobbering a user's edits on upgrade.
backup=('etc/genesi-ai-mode/advanced.conf' 'etc/genesi-ai-assist.conf')
source=()
sha256sums=()

package() {
    # Daemon + user-facing control CLI
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-aid"      "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-aid"
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-mode"  "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-ai-mode"
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-turbo"  "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-ai-turbo"
    # Spoken answers (Kokoro) and the hosted-model key. Separate tools
    # rather than flags on genesi-ai-mode: each owns a file, each can be
    # run and tested from a terminal, and Genesi Center reads both
    # through their --json rather than looking for the files itself.
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-voice"  "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-ai-voice"
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-key"  "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-ai-key"
    # Nightly autotuner: sweeps ubatch/flash-attn/KV/threads with llama-bench on
    # THIS hardware and writes the winning knobs to tune.json, which
    # genesi-ai-turbo reads as its defaults (env vars still override).
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-tune"  "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-ai-tune"

    # Standalone SNI tray applet (the AI Mode widget for non-Plasma sessions, i.e.
    # Hyprland + caelestia-shell). Started via exec-once from the caelestia
    # hyprland.conf; works in any StatusNotifierItem host.
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-tray"  "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-ai-tray"
    # .desktop matching the tray's app_id so xdg-desktop-portal can resolve its
    # app info (kills "App info not found for 'genesi-ai-tray'") and the SNI host
    # can read its icon/identity. NoDisplay — it's a background applet.
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-tray.desktop" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/applications/genesi-ai-tray.desktop"

    # systemd service
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-aid.service" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/system/genesi-aid.service"

    # Always-warm shared Turbo server (roadmap 2.8.11 Tier 1). Shipped but NOT
    # enabled by default — opt-in via advanced.conf `always_warm_turbo` (genesi-aid
    # starts/stops it) or `systemctl enable --now genesi-turbo.service`.
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-turbo.service" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/system/genesi-turbo.service"

    # Nightly autotuner service + timer (self-calibration). The timer is enabled
    # in .install; the service self-gates on AC + idle so it never steals the GPU.
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-tune.service" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/system/genesi-ai-tune.service"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-tune.timer" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/system/genesi-ai-tune.timer"

    # Runtime dir for user<->daemon IPC (world-writable, on tmpfs)
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-mode.tmpfiles.conf" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/genesi-ai-mode.conf"

    # Static sysctl tuning
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/99-genesi-ai.conf" \
        "$pkgdir/etc/sysctl.d/99-genesi-ai.conf"

    # Advanced opt-in tweaks (2.8.9) — shipped all-OFF + documented (see backup())
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/advanced.conf" \
        "$pkgdir/etc/genesi-ai-mode/advanced.conf"

    # Ollama defaults (only applies once Ollama is installed)
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-ollama.conf" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/system/ollama.service.d/10-genesi.conf"

    # pacman hook: auto-enable + start ollama.service when ollama is installed
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-ollama-enable.hook" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/libalpm/hooks/zz-genesi-ollama-enable.hook"

    # Plasma 6 widget (live dashboard + on/auto/off). Not auto-added to the
    # panel — the org.kde.plasma.icon launcher in the default layout already
    # provides the toggle UX; this is here for users who want the status view.
    install -dm755 "$pkgdir/usr/share/plasma/plasmoids/org.genesi.aimode"
    cp -r "${startdir}/plasmoid-aimode/"* \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/plasma/plasmoids/org.genesi.aimode/"

    # AI Mode Monitor — standalone Qt6/Kirigami dashboard app
    # Genesi AI Assist — the shared gate for passive helpers, plus the first
    # one (explain a failed command). Shipped inside genesi-ai-mode rather than
    # as its own package on purpose: this is already installed on every machine,
    # so the helper cannot repeat genesi-mesh's mistake of existing in the repo
    # but never reaching anyone.
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi_ai_assist.py" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/genesi_ai_assist.py"
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-explain" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-explain"
    # profile.d so interactive shells pick the hook up with no dotfile edits.
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-assist.sh" \
        "$pkgdir/etc/profile.d/genesi-assist.sh"
    # fish does not read profile.d. It is also Genesi's default interactive
    # shell, so without this file the helper did nothing for most users.
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-assist.fish" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/fish/vendor_conf.d/genesi-assist.fish"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-assist.conf" \
        "$pkgdir/etc/genesi-ai-assist.conf"

    # genesi-find — describe a file instead of naming it. The CLI is the single
    # source of truth; the window and the Dolphin entry are front ends over
    # `genesi-find --json`, the same split as genesi-sandboxes.
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-find" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-find"
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-find-ui" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-find-ui"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/find/genesi_find_gui.py" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/find/genesi_find_gui.py"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/find/Main.qml" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/find/Main.qml"
    # Shared UI kit pieces, bundled at build like every other Genesi app.
    for _fq in Theme I18n GButton; do
        install -Dm644 "${startdir}/../genesi-ui-kit/components/${_fq}.qml" \
            "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/find/${_fq}.qml"
    done
    # ...and the SAME result card the Monitor chat uses. Genesi Find answers
    # in two places; a file should not look like one thing in the chat and
    # another in the standalone window.
    for _fc in FileCard FIcon; do
        install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/${_fc}.qml" \
            "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/find/${_fc}.qml"
    done
    install -dm755 "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/find/icons"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/icons/"*.svg \
        -t "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/find/icons/"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/find/org.genesi.find.desktop" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/applications/org.genesi.find.desktop"
    # Dolphin: right-click a folder -> "Search with AI here", scoped to it.
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-find-here.desktop" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/kio/servicemenus/genesi-find-here.desktop"

    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/genesi_ai_monitor.py" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/genesi_ai_monitor.py"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/genesi_agent.py" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/genesi_agent.py"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/genesi_ai_quick.py" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/genesi_ai_quick.py"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/Main.qml" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/Main.qml"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/MeshPage.qml" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/MeshPage.qml"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/ChatPage.qml" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/ChatPage.qml"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/AdvisorPage.qml" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/AdvisorPage.qml"
    # Shared UI building blocks (brand palette + reusable visual components)
    # Theme + GlassCard come from the shared Genesi UI kit (one canonical source,
    # bundled in at build — see genesi-ui-kit/README.md for why it's not a dep).
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/../genesi-ui-kit/components/Theme.qml" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/Theme.qml"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/../genesi-ui-kit/components/GlassCard.qml" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/GlassCard.qml"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/../genesi-ui-kit/components/I18n.qml" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/I18n.qml"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/GaugeArc.qml" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/GaugeArc.qml"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/ChatBubble.qml" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/ChatBubble.qml"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/QuickChat.qml" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/QuickChat.qml"
    # Chat primitives: the Genesi Find result block, the empty-state
    # suggestion card and the pill. A QML file that is not installed is a
    # "Type unavailable" on the user machine and a blank page, which is
    # exactly how pkgrel 135 went out -- so these ship with the page.
    for _cq in FileCard SuggestCard GPill; do
        install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/${_cq}.qml" \
            "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/${_cq}.qml"
    done
    # Automations tab: the Forge Canvas adapted for PC-wide automation, plus its
    # self-contained graphite theme + primitives (no dep on the shared Theme.qml).
    for _aq in AutomationsPage AutomationCanvas AutomationConfigPanel CanvasNode \
               PaletteItem FIcon FCard GButton GToggle CanvasTheme; do
        install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/${_aq}.qml" \
            "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/${_aq}.qml"
    done
    # Shared, Qt-free Turbo control (used by the Monitor backend AND the daemon).
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/genesi_turbo_ctl.py" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/genesi_turbo_ctl.py"
    # Qt-free half of "build my workflow with AI": the node catalogue the model
    # writes against, and the validator that drops whatever it invents. Split
    # out of the Monitor so it can be tested without PySide6 installed - see
    # ci/workflow-generator-test.py.
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/genesi_workflow_gen.py" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/genesi_workflow_gen.py"
    # Crafted Genesi SVG icon set (chat avatars, mode badges, gauges, logo)
    for ic in "${startdir}/monitor/icons/"*.svg; do
        install -Dm644 "$ic" \
            "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/monitor/icons/$(basename "$ic")"
    done
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/monitor/genesi-ai-monitor" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-ai-monitor"
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/monitor/genesi-ai-quick" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-ai-quick"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/org.genesi.aimonitor.desktop" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/applications/org.genesi.aimonitor.desktop"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/org.genesi.aiquick.desktop" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/applications/org.genesi.aiquick.desktop"
    # Branded app icon (the Genesi leaf + monitoring pulse)
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/monitor/icons/logo.svg" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/genesi-ai-monitor.svg"

    # Cross-desktop Quick Chat startup + native shortcut registration. The
    # service covers systemd-aware Wayland compositors; XDG autostart covers
    # classic desktops. A per-user Unix socket makes duplicate starts harmless.
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-quick.desktop" \
        "$pkgdir/etc/xdg/autostart/genesi-ai-quick.desktop"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-quick.service" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/user/genesi-ai-quick.service"
    install -dm755 "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/user/graphical-session.target.wants"
    ln -s ../genesi-ai-quick.service \
        "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/user/graphical-session.target.wants/genesi-ai-quick.service"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi_ai_quick_shortcuts.py" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/genesi-ai-mode/genesi_ai_quick_shortcuts.py"
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-quick-shortcuts" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-ai-quick-shortcuts"

    # KWin desktop-effect trimmer (user session) + KDE autostart entry
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-kwin-helper" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-ai-kwin-helper"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-kwin-helper.desktop" \
        "$pkgdir/etc/xdg/autostart/genesi-ai-kwin-helper.desktop"

    # One-time CUDA-backend offer at login (NVIDIA + proprietary driver only).
    # Self-contained detection in the user session; opens the Monitor on click.
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-cuda-notify" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-ai-cuda-notify"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-ai-cuda-notify.desktop" \
        "$pkgdir/etc/xdg/autostart/genesi-ai-cuda-notify.desktop"

    # Automations background engine (per-user). Runs the Monitor's automation
    # graphs whether or not the window is open. Enabled for every user by a
    # default.target.wants symlink (works on Plasma, Hyprland, GNOME, Xfce,
    # Cinnamon, Budgie, LXDE, Cosmic, Niri — and a No-Desktop install).
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-automationd" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/local/bin/genesi-automationd"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-automationd.service" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/user/genesi-automationd.service"
    install -dm755 "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/user/default.target.wants"
    ln -s ../genesi-automationd.service \
        "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/user/default.target.wants/genesi-automationd.service"
}
